#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "8831d548-7068-5c95-940d-c572f229c23b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7901" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <\\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ (5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <(5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ 2 & <5-2x \\\\ 2x & <3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "ecf42887-e079-5c33-99f8-d59fb138cba9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7902" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}+3x & <0. \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{2}+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{2}+1)}{2{x}^{2}+1} & <\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "10d11958-5339-54b2-a468-fb97f0752a75" :question/math-academy-id "e-7903" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7.\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/id #uuid "684b599a-3f29-5978-8a2c-140186e56c29" :question/math-academy-id "e-7904" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x\\lt 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 \\\\ -x & >0 \\\\ -3x & >0 \\\\ 1-3x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <2 \\\\ (1-3x)\\cdot \\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <(1-3x)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+5 & <2-6x \\\\ 7x & <-3 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359005b3-e88f-525d-b35b-69efb7236dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286394} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2ece7c-8b84-502e-9197-fa70c2476266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286395} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3c7d12-b56d-5aa4-86ae-d91948a186e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286396} {:answer/id #uuid "e940f551-0f01-5c56-bc7e-5f2e91a85df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286397} {:answer/id #uuid "b806af84-e00a-50b7-83be-af5a35cf98d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e3c7d12-b56d-5aa4-86ae-d91948a186e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286396} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d05bf0-fece-5310-8126-59f79ab3416c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b5529ad-b1a2-552f-b12b-dc7e0d4b23e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-116263" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)\\le 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2)$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} & \\ge \\frac{0}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} \\\\ 4x+7 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -7 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6037e0d5-3e9e-553a-a31a-fca34ace9bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286401} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0c24b6-da29-5e68-80a0-2ed48756beb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286402} {:answer/id #uuid "89515b2b-7ab4-55df-b45b-43339edcff23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286403} {:answer/id #uuid "9984be31-6c2f-577e-90bf-8ba463b7f807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286404} {:answer/id #uuid "66128952-c996-5f21-857f-000e32c9d34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66128952-c996-5f21-857f-000e32c9d34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286405} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d1b9d0e-b2bd-5c3b-8fbd-578a1323f298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "079d429f-ce2e-5460-8272-295a0ca5238b" :question/math-academy-id "q-116265" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)<0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-4\\pi <2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi <-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } & >\\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } \\\\ 2x+9 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-9 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a6d0fe-4266-5911-9f6a-1613fd9c9f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286408} {:answer/id #uuid "06454c98-f362-50ab-993c-5dcad0b22d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286409} {:answer/id #uuid "56af529f-a298-5f7a-9543-557ef4148605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286410} {:answer/id #uuid "538f5ae6-3244-5de8-9404-b508749ce025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286411} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c2ab07-2afc-5bfe-899c-5af65296e448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5c2ab07-2afc-5bfe-899c-5af65296e448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286412} :answer-field/id #uuid "a72623f4-8836-53da-b3b4-39ef60d8cbcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "005b4d3e-ef8e-55a5-ae81-8ff2469130a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-116269" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }\\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi <-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\le (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ 2x+3 & \\le 2-7x \\\\ 9x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a73b86a-8a46-5a2b-85b9-0051d98cfa63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286415} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbed8db-34b1-5c4c-aa03-785fd6250075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5<x\\le 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286416} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ec6ca1-0a01-5908-8d0a-8c33e187da59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286417} {:answer/id #uuid "11f4aa14-d454-5fb1-adea-89a546012787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5<x\\le 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286418} {:answer/id #uuid "e840141a-d7a3-5182-a878-854ed50af58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e840141a-d7a3-5182-a878-854ed50af58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286419} :answer-field/id #uuid "df6cd855-1f71-5d02-a766-53d229f7c8c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4cf36d3-7f64-5689-9ba7-6939c71f4209" :question/math-academy-id "q-116273" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x-3}\\le 2,\\,x>5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x>5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >5 \\\\ x-3 & >5-3 \\\\ x-3 & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $x-3$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>5).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $x-3,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le 2 \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le (x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x & \\le 2x-6 \\\\ -x & \\le -6 \\\\ x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>5$ and $x\\ge 6.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\ge 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105ccb02-f200-5610-9836-8000a8369485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105ccb02-f200-5610-9836-8000a8369485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286422} :answer-field/id #uuid "07150b6e-9fbf-5711-9107-13bc6ec9ed0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b681efae-467e-55c9-95b3-911e72eb79c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b681efae-467e-55c9-95b3-911e72eb79c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286424} :answer-field/id #uuid "795f9410-1d80-5fd6-8047-1f2a772a4878" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "255c8902-af73-55ea-b1a3-dba865ae654b" :question/math-academy-id "q-328407" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2}\\le 4y.$\n\n$y$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator ${y}^{2}+2$ is positive for all values of $y.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${y}^{2}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le 4y \\\\ ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot 4y \\\\ 4{y}^{3}+11y-5 & \\le 4{y}^{3}+8y \\\\ 11y-5 & \\le 8y \\\\ 3y-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le \\,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2d580d-166e-5bf8-86d4-f06ca378bdad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d580d-166e-5bf8-86d4-f06ca378bdad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286427} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d94646-4631-5ab3-8ef9-039fca06c15d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb136138-89cf-5bc6-b9d4-4d5614064553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb136138-89cf-5bc6-b9d4-4d5614064553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286429} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0ac08d-0cdf-5354-be1f-b7db0ef51474" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b89b9610-96ff-53fd-8e92-9072d2639177" :question/math-academy-id "q-328412" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3}>\\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the denominator $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ is always positive. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,2\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\le 4.\n$$\n\nSo, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} & >\\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3)\\cdot \\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} & >(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3)\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} \\\\ 9-2x & >x-1 \\\\ -3x & >-10 \\\\ x & <\\,\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd083002-5050-5163-a223-294d5147c3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286432} {:answer/id #uuid "29fcbf67-9247-5f31-8d30-9598d5b47620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd083002-5050-5163-a223-294d5147c3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286432} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b2f213-ae3c-563e-b615-1b546b122cbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80557a9d-bbf8-5a1b-9960-7a61cd498a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80557a9d-bbf8-5a1b-9960-7a61cd498a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286435} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd11094a-3ed1-597e-9a23-c2730bd286c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44b485fa-793a-5558-8cff-318904682d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-328530" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{3x+4}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}>\\frac{x+10}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >\\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ 3x+4 & >x+10 \\\\ 2x & >6 \\\\ x & >\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60f560b-6869-558d-aaf5-a31e605e6e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b60f560b-6869-558d-aaf5-a31e605e6e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286438} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd0300c-4353-5fd8-a5c5-906841d58d7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c887eaad-9ebe-51d0-a898-e4d36619ca5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c887eaad-9ebe-51d0-a898-e4d36619ca5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286440} :answer-field/id #uuid "03434db9-ef05-5afa-9783-b37b122f05b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78731f9d-80d9-538e-a8e9-ae6bd9db97d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-328531" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}\\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator $|x|+{x}^{2}+1$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $|x|+{x}^{2}+1$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} & \\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ (|x|+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} & \\ge (|x|+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 2x+7 & \\ge x+4 \\\\ x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c5cc82-c496-59bb-ad2d-bbcb7db18e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286443} {:answer/id #uuid "47bbe85d-8350-50ae-b3ab-0c19ec6035bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c5cc82-c496-59bb-ad2d-bbcb7db18e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286443} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf071a7e-d2ea-5786-9787-eaf2cd870d2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c97e1c-f2a2-5d38-b9af-3a5d6cbeda7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c97e1c-f2a2-5d38-b9af-3a5d6cbeda7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286446} :answer-field/id #uuid "31302e44-9541-587b-adb2-ee39baf608ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1be8a95-00d3-5635-8d87-78e9d7b1b05f" :question/math-academy-id "q-328549" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9})(-7x-5)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ is always positive for all real numbers $x.$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9})(-7x-5)}{\\,2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\,} & <\\frac{0}{\\,2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\,} \\\\ -7x-5 & <0 \\\\ -7x & <5 \\\\ x & >\\,-\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74eaaf5c-3e1c-5883-a199-218c86b08473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74eaaf5c-3e1c-5883-a199-218c86b08473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286449} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa16a87-7fb5-5bad-8967-23387f1415f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18c72f0-3ac1-58a0-bb76-696d6918db58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a18c72f0-3ac1-58a0-bb76-696d6918db58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286451} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea00d19a-3df5-5c3c-88af-64b554e18bd6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "128fbafe-4df5-5561-876c-935b083d1854" :question/math-academy-id "q-328553" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$({e}^{2x}+{x}^{4})(5x-1)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since ${e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({e}^{2x}+{x}^{4})(5x-1)}{{e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}} & <\\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}} \\\\ 5x-1 & <0 \\\\ 5x & <1 \\\\ x & <\\,\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db9ff17-d044-5f0e-9887-e940f84feb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286454} {:answer/id #uuid "b49cb1de-3b06-509c-9c3b-a4814e68e5a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3db9ff17-d044-5f0e-9887-e940f84feb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286454} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d9cf8b-25a0-5dda-a977-53b0a291a649" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016e0125-ac97-52e9-8332-160584d63b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016e0125-ac97-52e9-8332-160584d63b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286457} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c30737-e768-5b80-835a-92a1633f5746" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe32e3f7-bd8c-5e0b-a97f-85bc71bd7ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe32e3f7-bd8c-5e0b-a97f-85bc71bd7ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286459} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c055130-084b-5f91-91e1-eee1f253b56f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9b27759-e26c-5938-bbc4-e59752cda93b" :question/math-academy-id "q-329163" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-1 & >2 \\\\ |x-1| & >2 \\\\ |x-1|-1 & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $|x-1|-1$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $|x-1|-1,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (|x-1|-1)\\cdot \\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge (|x-1|-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 8-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality again since we divided by a negative number ($-2$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x\\le 4.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n3<x\\le 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdbe888-6d00-5139-b424-b4378f4e774a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 < x < \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156737} {:answer/id #uuid "a9350c50-68ba-5235-b532-72562a07406c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 < x < 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156738} {:answer/id #uuid "bf87081e-0c4f-5601-a029-f5e86af1d101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156739} {:answer/id #uuid "45a87c60-7da5-5b7c-8371-1f060b294205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156740} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3e006a-7310-52d5-bb92-33e7abb66360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a87c60-7da5-5b7c-8371-1f060b294205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156740} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a302d4-a2cf-52a7-8e7a-eae757ccb440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69f3af74-324d-5931-85f9-57e1b17f2095" :question/math-academy-id "q-89117" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{1-x}>4,\\,x>2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ -x & <-2 \\\\ 1-x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-x$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-x,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & >4 \\\\ (1-x)\\cdot \\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & <(1-x)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 3x+2 & <4-4x \\\\ 7x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there are no numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x<\\frac{2}{7}.$ So, the inequality has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe716a13-88f7-54d5-b359-890b5ed2c61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286462} {:answer/id #uuid "423ac134-8235-512e-9bd9-30c144e432af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286463} {:answer/id #uuid "50acefe7-4585-5f80-bb57-04125ec2c200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286464} {:answer/id #uuid "e8de4e92-426f-57a5-b02e-2df3760bf47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286465} {:answer/id #uuid "303e801e-9848-51f5-8c96-3e6ed87c6185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8de4e92-426f-57a5-b02e-2df3760bf47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286465} :answer-field/id #uuid "034bd514-51dc-5a27-bef2-d13502d75889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b407ad42-5458-597b-a7e6-8e921a6fa978" :question/math-academy-id "q-89562" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4)(x-1)\\le 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4$ is always positive. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {cos}^{2}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,4\\le 3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4\\le 7.\n$$\n\nSince $3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4)(x-1)}{3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4} & \\le \\frac{0}{3{cos}^{2}⁡x+4} \\\\ x-1 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f7eacd-7832-5a05-8044-eeb1810c5d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156785} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa40aa9-ccf0-5db8-8469-3f5024e4aa53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156786} {:answer/id #uuid "049cd968-95bb-50d5-b4d8-17064bba4ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 < x < \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156787} {:answer/id #uuid "1a80185b-da34-57fb-aec9-3cd650570c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156788} {:answer/id #uuid "77233934-2e1e-5fdd-b806-350e5617dff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 < x < 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfa40aa9-ccf0-5db8-8469-3f5024e4aa53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156786} :answer-field/id #uuid "567334e6-b7a9-58ee-84df-6c3d7281833f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a328572c-0653-5db2-9ac5-0c926d0fcf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-89571" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-2 & >3-2 \\\\ x-2 & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{2x-3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2x-3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x>\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33474b8d-0dbc-5222-8ed4-185e8e0d9424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286469} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a7834c-bd5b-5b4d-8a7c-7853cf933069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286470} {:answer/id #uuid "f410ada1-238b-5d78-a359-e34f2e22117a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\le x\\le -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286471} {:answer/id #uuid "406685b0-9c67-5f98-a966-e4f978643dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\le x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286472} {:answer/id #uuid "1346a1ab-58c5-522c-a120-abb3e35383ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33474b8d-0dbc-5222-8ed4-185e8e0d9424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286469} :answer-field/id #uuid "f69aa5b4-3b65-5ed1-8adf-c152a4b0543c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c3d094e-2960-5478-a6a7-e8c041fc7903" :question/math-academy-id "q-89572" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x+3)\\ge 0,\\,x<-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x<-1,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-1 \\\\ 2x & <-2 \\\\ 2x-1 & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2x-1$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<-1).$ So, we can divide both sides by $2x-1,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-1)(x+3) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2x-1)(x+3)}{2x-1} & \\le \\frac{0}{2x-1} \\\\ x+3 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\le -3$ and $x<-1.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\le -3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e36259d-0ad2-54db-bb24-8009564c8044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286476} {:answer/id #uuid "9697af63-e10d-5d4a-9017-feecd9bf56ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286477} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb38c30-f6dd-524f-904c-cc60ea40aa8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286478} {:answer/id #uuid "900974c1-5aec-5d04-bf41-25c4b9a237f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286479} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1516af-ec1e-514a-a033-49dc4ceff642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9697af63-e10d-5d4a-9017-feecd9bf56ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286477} :answer-field/id #uuid "78d426cb-1dd7-5e7e-9b01-c4e0b07ed868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62752f35-a978-5f90-a8a9-d9ceb9f7ef79" :question/math-academy-id "q-89651" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2}\\le 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator ${x}^{4}+2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${x}^{4}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{4}+3x}{{x}^{4}+2} & \\le ({x}^{4}+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+3x & \\le 2{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ 3x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d935736c-b3cd-52d5-b4e5-fd6b353bb8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156791} {:answer/id #uuid "000323d3-0998-5be8-a4a7-65369409380f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156792} {:answer/id #uuid "0414a6c9-87a1-5969-9333-8b2914e4317c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge \\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156793} {:answer/id #uuid "69e31be8-12e1-581c-afb0-b754dae4b50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156794} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4c66c6-15af-5e91-9668-45ff9bc6e2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000323d3-0998-5be8-a4a7-65369409380f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a8af44-7dd5-5450-9025-6e1b095227ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6458b71f-52d7-59f3-bc00-5ea668785e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-89658" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x}\\le 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the given inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{2x} & \\le 0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}(4x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{2x}(4x-5)}{{e}^{2x}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ 4x-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4x & \\le 5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85520716-87a8-575a-acc3-427e49492210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286483} {:answer/id #uuid "0269a33a-02b9-56bd-a588-33febba6040e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286484} {:answer/id #uuid "63ea2566-3672-5541-ac3e-2f3fb1b49924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286485} {:answer/id #uuid "49557046-514a-58a7-82df-49d85d28d121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286486} {:answer/id #uuid "5e202dd0-601d-575c-bed7-8792a3bbc978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49557046-514a-58a7-82df-49d85d28d121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286486} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3bc5f9c-0057-5099-b478-272ba2eea899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f44f2990-d5b3-5df2-b67d-fb78ccc8bd0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-89661" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6{x}^{5}+18x>3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all terms to the left-hand side and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}+18x & >3x \\\\ 6{x}^{5}+15x & >0 \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{4}+5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2{x}^{4}+5$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{4}+5)}{2{x}^{4}+5} & >\\frac{0}{2{x}^{4}+5} \\\\ 3x & >0 \\\\ x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40821dba-62fa-5b78-80f7-9eaf4ed4d6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286490} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc766c4-dad9-5360-b2a6-b52babaa635f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286491} {:answer/id #uuid "c185b79c-f9ae-59e9-9dba-19f4a71dcfd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286492} {:answer/id #uuid "13926da6-f8ea-5f38-8be1-dcca2ea326f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286493} {:answer/id #uuid "2623d4a6-eae9-5f22-a132-e97ed6d5b3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13926da6-f8ea-5f38-8be1-dcca2ea326f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286493} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45068e9-4574-5c00-b993-1b41571b46b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92a095d0-ce8e-56cc-ae98-0a56b2f75ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89671" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3}>\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {sin}^{2}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,-3\\le 2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3\\le -1.\n$$\n\nSince $2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3$ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} & >\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} \\\\ (2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} & <(2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3)\\cdot \\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x-3} \\\\ x+2 & <4-3x \\\\ 4x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a9ee56f-95d0-53ce-bbd1-95404c89101d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286497} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac30744-c336-593a-9ab2-ca17f6f846d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286498} {:answer/id #uuid "96abff79-1dc0-5283-a690-0b9bca310e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286499} {:answer/id #uuid "5f794266-ff7e-5a15-864a-bc9e08bd3248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286500} {:answer/id #uuid "fb61ae1b-ccf1-5aac-92ab-46304dcc136a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb61ae1b-ccf1-5aac-92ab-46304dcc136a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286501} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e40c40-a1b3-5e74-899f-23b487ded6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1849fc0b-525b-5c23-a604-123f9d9d6807" :question/math-academy-id "q-89676" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)<0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $-3{x}^{2}-4$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-3{x}^{2}-4)(x-1)}{-3{x}^{2}-4} & >\\frac{0}{-3{x}^{2}-4} \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
